= Description logic =

== Overview ==
'''Description logics''' ('''DLs''') are a family of logic-based [[Knowledge representation|knowledge-representation]] formalisms used to describe concepts/classes, relations/roles and individuals while supporting well-defined automated reasoning tasks.

Description logics influenced ontology languages such as [[Web Ontology Language|OWL]].

Different DLs make different trade-offs between expressive power and computational complexity.

== Concepts, roles and individuals ==
A typical description logic represents:

'''Concepts''' — unary classes/categories such as Person.

'''Roles''' — binary relations such as hasChild.

'''Individuals''' — named objects such as Alice.

Example:

<pre>
Parent ≡ Person ⊓ ∃hasChild.Person
</pre>

informally defines a Parent as a Person who has at least one Person as a child.

== Constructors ==
Description logics build complex concepts from simpler ones.

Common constructors include:
* conjunction/intersection;
* disjunction/union;
* negation/complement;
* existential restriction;
* universal restriction;
* number restrictions;
* nominals;
* inverse roles.

Each DL supports a particular subset.

The supported constructors affect both expressivity and reasoning cost.

== TBox and ABox ==
A common distinction is:

'''TBox''' — terminological knowledge about concepts and roles.

Example:

<pre>
Dog ⊑ Mammal
</pre>

'''ABox''' — assertions about individuals.

Example:

<pre>
Dog(Fido)
</pre>

From both, a reasoner can infer:

<pre>
Mammal(Fido)
</pre>

== Subsumption ==
Concept A is subsumed by concept B when every instance of A must also be an instance of B.

Notation:

<pre>
A ⊑ B
</pre>

Reasoners can compute subclass hierarchies by testing subsumption.

This process is often called classification.

== Satisfiability ==
A concept is satisfiable if it can have at least one instance in some model of the ontology.

An ontology is consistent if its axioms have at least one model.

A contradictory set of axioms can make a class unsatisfiable even when no individual has been asserted to belong to it.

== Instance checking ==
Instance checking asks whether:

<pre>
O ⊨ C(a)
</pre>

That is: does ontology O entail that individual a is an instance of concept C?

This can involve several axioms, not only an explicit type assertion.

== Open-world semantics ==
DLs used for OWL typically use open-world semantics.

Missing information is not false.

This means an incomplete record does not automatically violate an ontology merely because a property is absent.

Closed-world validation requires additional mechanisms.

== Expressivity and decidability ==
Unrestricted first-order logic has undecidable general reasoning problems.

Description logics restrict language structure to obtain decidable reasoning for useful fragments.

Adding constructors can increase worst-case complexity.

Language design therefore balances:
* modeling power;
* decidability;
* computational complexity.

== Historical lineage ==
DLs developed from semantic-network and frame-based systems such as KL-ONE.

Early systems exposed ambiguity in informal "is-a" and slot inheritance.

DL research responded by giving concept languages explicit logical semantics and reasoning procedures.

== OWL connection ==
OWL 2 DL's direct semantics is closely connected to SROIQ.

OWL profiles correspond to restricted fragments designed for scalable classes of reasoning.

Description logics provide much of the formal theory behind these language choices.

== Epistemic notes ==
A description-logic reasoner proves consequences relative to its axioms and semantics. It does not determine whether those axioms correctly describe the real domain.

